{"step_index":486,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-26T07:34:36Z","thinking":"**Observing a Visual Detail**\n\nOkay, I've started by taking a look at `intro_act12_landing_1787729676186.jpg`. I'm focusing on the visual details. Specifically, I see Bé Bin sitting with a seatbelt fastened, the overhead sign illuminated, and the airport runway visible outside. Now, I'm shifting gears and looking into the Activity UI flow.\n\n\n**Defining the Activity Flow**\n\nI've outlined the eight stages of the activity, starting with preparation and ending with baggage claim. Each stage will be clearly labeled, such as \"Kiểm tra an ninh\" for security checks. I'm also planning to incorporate a sub-progress indicator for each stage, using bullet points to represent completion or current status, providing visual feedback to the user.\n\n\n**Structuring the Activity Views**\n\nI'm now focusing on structuring the two primary activity views. I've broken down the `.activity-intro-view`, noting the top badge format, activity name display, and the use of a parent prompt with an accompanying illustration. The `.activity-gameplay-view` will present a focused task, feedback banner, and a \"Continue\" button linking to the next activity's intro screen. I'm keen to implement these in the code now.\n\n\n**Analyzing Activity Data**\n\nOkay, I'm now digging into the data structure for the 13 activities. I've been reviewing the javascript `ACTIVITIES` object. Specifically, I'm taking a close look at the structure and content for each activity. I see the stage, activity number, title, speaker, intro text, and intro art. Now, I'm analyzing the data.\n\n\n**Examining Activity Details**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on activity data, especially activities 5 through 10. I see that activity 5 focuses on security and activity 6 focuses on finding the correct gate. I'm noting the stage, activity number, title, speaker, intro text, and introductory art. I'm paying attention to how the intro art corresponds to the activity's introduction text, such as in the seatbelt activity.\n\n\n**Reviewing Activity Data**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the specific data for activities 10 through 13. I'm focusing on the dialogue and visual pairings. For instance, activity 10 has the prompt \"Trên máy bay mình nói nhỏ vừa đủ nghe nhé.\" matched with the image of Bin whispering. Similarly, I'm analyzing how intro art and text align for activities 11 (\"Bin thu chân gọn lại để không chạm ghế phía trước nhé!\"), 12 (\"Máy bay đang chạy vào chỗ đỗ. Bin vẫn ngồi yên và giữ dây an toàn nhé!\"), and 13 (\"Con tìm chiếc vali đỏ có hình gấu của Bin nhé!\").\n\n\n**Detailing Activity Gameplay**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in the specifics of activities 4, 7, and 12. Activity 4 involves an X-ray scanner gameplay with objects to identify. I see activity 7 focus on seatbelt fastening using tap interactions. Activity 12 has gameplay centered around waiting for the plane to stop and unbuckling the seatbelt. I am looking into how to make the gameplay intuitive, and the completion feedback engaging.\n\n\n**Refining Activity 12 Gameplay**\n\nI've been working on Activity 12, the landing sequence. The initial state will show Bé Bin with a fastened seatbelt while the plane is still taxiing. The seatbelt release button is disabled, represented by \"⏳ Máy bay đang chạy vào bãi đỗ...\". After 2.6 seconds, the plane stops and the seatbelt sign turns off, enabling the button with \"🔓 Máy bay đã dừng hẳn — Mở dây an toàn\". Upon interaction, the seatbelt opens (\"TÁCH!\") and the visual changes to `bin_seated_plane`. Feedback is given: \"Máy bay dừng hẳn rồi. Bây giờ mình mới mở dây nhé!\" and a \"TIẾP TỤC\" button advances to Activity 13, which is the \"Find the Suitcase\" activity. I'm now examining the integration of completion feedback.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"/* ==============================================================